ave-now-raised-18506/articleshow/134292166.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-16T22:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134292216,imgsize-587575.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Greenland, Antarctica have lost 11,309 billion tonnes of ice since 1979, raising sea levels by 31.4 mm]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134291522.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Greenland and Antarctica have lost 11,309 billion tonnes of ice since 1979, raising global sea levels by 31.4 millimetres, a new assessment finds. Faster-flowing glaciers accounted for 84% of the loss, while surface melting contributed 16%. Scientists say the findings highlight the long-term impact of warming oceans on polar ice sheets.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/earth/greenland-and-antarctica-have-lost-11309-billion-tonnes-of-ice-between-1979-and-2023-raising-sea-levels-by-31-4-mm-glaciers-drive-84-of-loss-surface-melting-16/articleshow/134291522.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/earth/greenland-and-antarctica-have-lost-11309-billion-tonnes-of-ice-between-1979-and-2023-raising-sea-levels-by-31-4-mm-glaciers-drive-84-of-loss-surface-melting-16/articleshow/134291522.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-16T21:16:49+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134291612,imgsize-112925.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[We are US citizens: Meet Dhairya Gupta and Rahul Kodali who went to watch a football match. Now their faces are target of racist rants]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofi